  "decision": "Two alternatives, as KerML 8.2.4.1.6 states them. The distinction between them is the `member` keyword that TypeFeatureMember carries and OwnedFeatureMember does not, and that keyword is the whole of the concrete-syntax difference.",
  "notes": "The clause and the Xtext agree exactly.\n\nFOR A PARSER AUTHOR: the two alternatives are distinguishable, but not at the first token, because both open with the nullable MemberPrefix. The decision point is the token after the optional visibility indicator: `member` selects TypeFeatureMember, anything that can start a FeatureElement selects OwnedFeatureMember. The two are otherwise the same shape.\n\nWHY THE DISTINCTION EXISTS, as far as this production says: the clause gives the two alternatives different metaclasses \u2014 TypeFeatureMember is an OwningMembership, OwnedFeatureMember is a FeatureMembership \u2014 so the keyword changes which relationship the member establishes. That is abstract syntax and belongs to whoever builds the element; recorded here only because it is why one keyword is worth a production.\n\nThe empty corpus_instances is expected: this is a pure alternation contributing no terminal of its own, so corpus text is attributable to whichever alternative matched.",
